Floragrit

Creative Catalyst: The genre was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule, salvaged from the magnetic sludge of a 1984 tropical expedition.

Floragrit sounds like a sun-drenched greenhouse collapsing into a high-speed digital processor. It replaces the grime genre’s aggressive nihilism with an unhinged, botanical optimism. The iconic Fairlight CMI isn’t used for "futuristic" pads, but is instead pushed to its breaking point to simulate the sound of growing vines and decaying sunlight. It is brittle, fast, and aggressively happy, like a frantic bird singing inside a crashed spaceship.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 145–155 BPM; a jagged, syncopated breakbeat shuffle that hiccups rhythmically, mirroring the tape-warp fluctuations of the source material.
*   Harmonic Palette: Major-key tropical dissonance (Mixolydian-Lydian hybrids) layered over bit-crushed, gritty industrial noise-floors.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Bio-Bit-Crushing:* Running Fairlight orchestral samples through a recursive feedback loop of plant-tissue moisture sensors to modulate bit-depth.
       Tape-Hiss Percussion:* Stripping the high-end frequency artifacts of the original "corrupted" cassette to serve as the high-hats and snares.
       Harmonic Grafting:* The forced synthesis of acoustic 70s Brazilian percussion transients directly onto digital 80s wavetables.
*   Dynamics: Start-stop bursts of pure euphoria that suddenly collapse into grainy, low-fidelity tape-drag segments before erupting into chaotic, jungle-inspired rhythmic cascades.

Scene & Origin Lore
Floragrit emerged in the derelict botanical glasshouses of a fictionalized post-industrial London, where urban explorers discovered the "Time-Capsule Cassette." The subculture—known as "Grafters"—fashions their gear out of repurposed 80s sampling hardware and humidifiers. They dress in transparent PVC raincoats filled with dried jungle moss and wear circuitry-patterned silk shirts imported from vintage markets. Their rituals involve "blooming," where they plug their gear into the root systems of abandoned orchids, allowing the bio-electrical output to influence the Fairlight’s filter settings during live improvisation.

Typical Instruments: Fairlight CMI (Series III), modified surdo drums with contact microphones, granular-synthesized steel-string guitar, and heavily sampled 80s radio interference.

For Fans Of: Tom Ze, Dizzee Rascal, Autechre, and Sergio Mendes.

